# Cursor AI Agent Rules for Scientific Data Analysis
# ============================================================================
# This file configures the AI agent's behavior for this R-based scientific 
# data analysis project. The agent should follow these guidelines when 
# assisting with code, analysis, and documentation.

## Project Context
# ============================================================================
# This is a scientific research project focused on:
# - Clinical trial data analysis (bladder cancer, ctDNA)
# - Survival analysis and time-to-event outcomes
# - Statistical modeling (Cox regression, GAM, GLM)
# - Reproducible research with R Markdown
# - Publication-quality figures and tables

## Directory Structure
# ============================================================================
# - scripts/        → Standalone R scripts (.R files)
# - rmarkdown/     → R Markdown reports (.Rmd files)
# - data/          → Data files (.csv, .xlsx, .rds)
# - outputs/       → Generated HTML outputs (ignored by git)
# - figures/       → Generated plots (ignored by git)
# - documents/     → PDFs, Word docs, presentations (ignored by git)
# - python/        → Python utility scripts
# - docs/          → Documentation and guides (.md)
# - temp/          → Temporary files (ignored by git)

## Code Style & Best Practices
# ============================================================================

### R Code Style
- Use tidyverse packages (dplyr, tidyr, ggplot2, readr, readxl) as primary tools
- Follow tidyverse style guide: https://style.tidyverse.org/
- Use meaningful variable names (snake_case preferred)
- Add comments explaining statistical methods, not just code mechanics
- Always load libraries at the top of scripts/Rmd files
- Use `here::here()` or relative paths from project root for file paths

### Data Handling
- Always check data structure with `glimpse()`, `str()`, or `summary()` after loading
- Handle missing data explicitly - document assumptions about missingness
- Use `readxl::read_excel()` for Excel files, `readr::read_csv()` for CSV
- Save processed data as `.rds` files in `data/` directory when intermediate steps are needed
- Never hardcode file paths - use relative paths or `here::here()`

### Statistical Analysis
- For survival analysis: use `survival` and `survminer` packages
- For Cox models: always check proportional hazards assumption
- For time-dependent analysis: use appropriate landmark or time-dependent Cox models
- Document statistical assumptions and check them (normality, linearity, etc.)
- Report effect sizes, confidence intervals, and p-values appropriately
- Use appropriate multiple comparison corrections when needed

### Reproducibility
- Set random seeds: `set.seed(123)` at the start of analyses
- Use `sessionInfo()` or `renv` for package version control
- Document data cleaning steps clearly
- Use R Markdown for all reports - never write analysis code that can't be reproduced
- Include data dictionary or variable descriptions in documentation

### Visualization
- Use ggplot2 for all plots - follow publication standards
- Save figures to `figures/` directory
- Use appropriate themes: `theme_minimal()`, `theme_classic()`, or `theme_bw()` for publications
- Ensure text is readable (font size, contrast)
- Include proper axis labels, titles, and legends
- For survival curves: use `ggsurvplot()` from survminer
- Export high-resolution figures (300+ DPI for publications)

### R Markdown Best Practices
- Use meaningful chunk names: `{r data-loading}` not `{r chunk1}`
- Set chunk options appropriately:
  - `echo = FALSE` for data loading/cleaning
  - `eval = FALSE` for code you want to show but not run
  - `warning = FALSE, message = FALSE` for cleaner output
  - `fig.width = 7, fig.height = 5` for consistent figure sizing
- Use `include = FALSE` for setup chunks
- Organize Rmd files logically: Setup → Data Loading → Analysis → Results → Discussion

## Common Tasks & Patterns
# ============================================================================

### Loading Data
```r
library(readxl)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)

# Load data
data <- read_excel("data/filename.xlsx") %>%
  clean_names() %>%  # from janitor package
  mutate(...)  # data cleaning steps
```

### Date Handling
- Use `lubridate` package for date operations
- Handle Excel date serials appropriately (see `as_date_safe()` in shared setup)
- Always validate date ranges (e.g., clinical trial dates should be reasonable)

### Survival Analysis Pattern
```r
library(survival)
library(survminer)

# Fit Cox model
cox_model <- coxph(Surv(time, event) ~ predictor + covariates, data = data)

# Check assumptions
cox.zph(cox_model)

# Create survival plot
ggsurvplot(survfit(cox_model), data = data, ...)
```

### Creating Tables
- Use `knitr::kable()` or `kableExtra::kable_styling()` for publication tables
- Use `gtsummary::tbl_summary()` for descriptive statistics tables
- Export tables to Excel using `writexl::write_xlsx()` when needed

## Error Handling & Debugging
# ============================================================================
- When errors occur, suggest checking:
  1. Data structure and types
  2. Missing values
  3. Variable names (case sensitivity, typos)
  4. Package versions
- Use `browser()` for interactive debugging when needed
- Always test code on a small subset of data first

## Documentation
# ============================================================================
- Add comments explaining WHY, not just WHAT
- Document statistical methods used
- Explain data transformations and their rationale
- Note any deviations from standard approaches
- Include references to statistical methods when appropriate

## File Naming Conventions
# ============================================================================
- Scripts: descriptive names like `ctDNA_survival_analysis.R`
- R Markdown: descriptive names like `Question1_Baseline_ctDNA_Prognosis.Rmd`
- Data files: keep original names when possible, or use descriptive names
- Figures: descriptive names like `survival_curve_by_group.png`

## Package Recommendations
# ============================================================================
# Core packages to use:
# - tidyverse (dplyr, tidyr, ggplot2, readr, readxl, stringr)
# - survival, survminer (survival analysis)
# - lubridate (dates)
# - janitor (data cleaning)
# - knitr, rmarkdown (reports)
# - kableExtra, gtsummary (tables)
# - here (file paths)

## What NOT to Do
# ============================================================================
- Don't use base R when tidyverse has a better solution
- Don't hardcode file paths
- Don't skip data validation steps
- Don't create plots without proper labels
- Don't run analyses without checking assumptions
- Don't commit generated outputs (HTML, PDFs, figures) - they're in .gitignore
- Don't modify data files in place - always create new processed versions

## When Writing Code
# ============================================================================
- Always think about reproducibility
- Consider edge cases (missing data, outliers, etc.)
- Write code that's readable by others (future you included)
- Use functions for repeated operations
- Keep scripts focused on single tasks when possible
- Use R Markdown for complete analyses that need documentation

## Output Expectations
# ============================================================================
- Code should be production-ready, not just working
- Comments should explain statistical reasoning
- Variable names should be self-documenting
- Code should follow the project's existing patterns
- When creating new files, place them in the appropriate directory

## Special Considerations for Clinical Data
# ============================================================================
- Be careful with patient identifiers - never commit PHI
- Handle censoring appropriately in survival analyses
- Consider competing risks when relevant
- Document any data transformations that might affect clinical interpretation
- Use appropriate statistical tests for clinical endpoints (e.g., log-rank for survival)
